/* ============================ 4. SCIPY & STATISTICS ============================ */
{ num: 4, emoji: '🧪', title: 'SciPy & Statistical Testing', layer: 'Stats',
  tagline: 'Distributions, hypothesis tests, optimisation, sparse matrices — and the p-value question you WILL be asked.',
  apps: ['A/B test analysis', 'sklearn returns scipy.sparse from encoders', 'curve fitting / optimisation'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>NumPy gives you arrays; <b>SciPy</b> is the scientific toolbox bolted on top: <code>stats</code> (distributions + tests), <code>optimize</code>, <code>sparse</code>, <code>interpolate</code>, <code>signal</code>, <code>spatial</code>, <code>linalg</code>.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>NumPy is the workbench; SciPy is the drawer of instruments.</b> A stats kit, an optimiser, a signal bench — each a specialised tool that assumes you already have the raw numbers.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>from scipy import stats\nstats.norm.cdf(1.96)        # 0.975  — area left of 1.96 under a standard normal\nstats.norm.ppf(0.975)      # 1.96   — the inverse\nt, p = stats.ttest_ind(group_a, group_b)</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 SciPy docs', 'https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>A distribution object is <b>a shape you can query</b>: <code>.pdf(x)</code> (density), <code>.cdf(x)</code> (area up to x), <code>.ppf(q)</code> (the x for a given left-area = a quantile), <code>.rvs(n)</code> (random samples).</p>' +
    '<pre><code>stats.binom(n=10, p=0.3).pmf(4)      # P(exactly 4 successes)\nstats.t(df=20).ppf(0.975)            # critical t value\nstats.describe(x)                    # mean, var, skew, kurtosis\nstats.skew(x), stats.kurtosis(x)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A frozen distribution is a die you can interrogate before rolling it.</b> "What is P(exactly 4)?", "What is the value with 95% below it?" — all answerable without a single sample.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 scipy.stats', 'https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/stats.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The tests every DS interview expects you to name:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>two group means, numeric      → stats.ttest_ind (Welch: equal_var=False); paired → ttest_rel\n3+ group means                → stats.f_oneway  (ANOVA)\ncategorical vs categorical    → stats.chi2_contingency(crosstab)\nnon-normal / ranks            → stats.mannwhitneyu, stats.wilcoxon\nnormality check               → stats.shapiro\nequal variance check          → stats.levene\ncorrelation                   → stats.pearsonr (linear), stats.spearmanr (monotonic)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>A/B test: 2x2 table of variant x converted. <code>chi2, p, _, _ = stats.chi2_contingency(table)</code>. Report the p-value <b>and</b> the absolute lift <b>and</b> a confidence interval on the difference in proportions — significance without effect size is not an answer.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Statistics in SciPy', 'https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/tutorial/stats.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The rest of SciPy you actually touch, plus the gotchas:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>optimize.minimize(fn, x0), optimize.curve_fit(model, x, y), optimize.linprog(...)\nsparse: OneHotEncoder / TfidfVectorizer return CSR matrices — never .toarray() a huge one\nspatial: cKDTree for fast nearest-neighbour; distance.cdist for pairwise distances\n\nGOTCHAS:\n• one-sided vs two-sided (alternative=\'greater\'); p_one ≈ p_two / 2\n• default Student t assumes equal variance → prefer equal_var=False (Welch)\n• MULTIPLE TESTING: 20 tests at α=0.05 → ~1 false positive by chance.\n  Correct: Bonferroni (α/m) or Benjamini-Hochberg FDR (statsmodels.stats.multitest)\n• p-value depends on n: a trivial effect becomes "significant" at huge n → always report effect size + CI\n• power = P(detect a real effect); design n up front (statsmodels power module)</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 scipy.sparse', 'https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/sparse.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: What is a p-value?\nA: The probability of observing data at least this extreme IF the null hypothesis were true.\n   It is NOT P(null is true), and not the probability the result is due to chance in general.\n\nQ: Type I vs Type II error?\nA: Type I = false positive (reject a true null; rate = α). Type II = false negative (fail to\n   reject a false null; rate = β). Power = 1 − β.\n\nQ: Which test? two group conversion rates / two group means non-normal / paired before-after / 3 groups.\nA: chi-square (or two-proportion z) / Mann-Whitney U / paired t-test (or Wilcoxon) / one-way ANOVA.\n\nQ: A/B test: 5.0% → 5.3% conversion, n = 1000 each. Significant?\nA: Roughly: SE ≈ sqrt(2 * 0.05*0.95 / 1000) ≈ 0.0097 → z ≈ 0.31 → p ≈ 0.75. Not significant,\n   and the test is badly under-powered for a 0.3pp effect (need ~n≈100k/arm).\n\nQ: Why does OneHotEncoder return a sparse matrix?\nA: One-hot of high-cardinality categoricals is 99%+ zeros; CSR stores only non-zeros,\n   saving memory and speeding up linear models.\n\nQ: You ran 30 comparisons. What is the risk and the fix?\nA: Family-wise error inflation (~1 − 0.95^30 ≈ 79% chance of ≥1 false positive).\n   Fix: Bonferroni or FDR (Benjamini-Hochberg) correction.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 statsmodels multiple testing', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/generated/statsmodels.stats.multitest.multipletests.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Which statement about a p-value of 0.03 is correct?', opts: ['There is a 3% chance the null hypothesis is true', 'If the null were true, data this extreme (or more) would occur 3% of the time', 'The effect size is 3%', 'The result will replicate 97% of the time'], ok: 1, why: 'A p-value is P(data this extreme | H0). It says nothing directly about P(H0), the effect size, or replication probability.' },
    { q: 'You want to test whether "device type" (mobile/desktop) is associated with "purchased" (yes/no). Correct test?', opts: ['Independent t-test', 'Chi-square test of independence on the 2x2 contingency table', 'ANOVA', 'Pearson correlation'], ok: 1, why: 'Both variables are categorical, so a chi-square test of independence (or an equivalent two-proportion z-test) on the crosstab is appropriate.' },
    { q: 'You run 40 A/B metrics at alpha = 0.05 and 3 come back "significant". What is the concern?', opts: ['Nothing, 3 wins is great', 'Multiple comparisons: ~2 false positives expected by chance; apply Bonferroni or FDR correction', 'You need a bigger effect size', 'Chi-square was the wrong test'], ok: 1, why: 'At alpha 0.05 across 40 independent tests you expect about 2 significant results even if nothing is real. Correct with Bonferroni (alpha/m) or Benjamini-Hochberg FDR before celebrating.' } ] },

/* ============================ 5. STATSMODELS ============================ */
{ num: 5, emoji: '📑', title: 'statsmodels', layer: 'Stats',
  tagline: 'Regression the statistician\'s way: coefficients with p-values, confidence intervals, and a full diagnostic report.',
  apps: ['"which factors drive churn, and how sure are we?"', 'econometrics / causal-ish analysis', 'A/B tests with covariates'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>scikit-learn tells you <i>what</i> it predicts. <b>statsmodels</b> tells you <i>why</i> and <i>how confident</i> — every coefficient with a standard error, t-statistic, p-value and confidence interval, plus a summary table you can put in a report.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A car service report, not just "it runs".</b> Every reading listed, each flagged in-spec or out-of-spec. That is <code>results.summary()</code>.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>import statsmodels.formula.api as smf\nm = smf.ols(\'revenue ~ spend + tenure + C(plan)\', data=df).fit()\nm.summary()          # coefficients, p-values, R^2, F-stat, diagnostics\nm.params, m.pvalues, m.conf_int()</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 statsmodels docs', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Two APIs for the same models:</p>' +
    '<pre><code># array API — you build the design matrix\nimport statsmodels.api as sm\nX = sm.add_constant(X)          # &lt;- add the intercept column yourself!\nsm.OLS(y, X).fit()\n\n# formula API (R-style) — patsy builds it for you\nsmf.ols(\'y ~ x1 + x2 + x1:x2 + C(cat)\', data=df).fit()\nsmf.logit(\'churn ~ price + tenure\', data=df).fit()</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b><code>C(cat)</code> = "treat this as buckets, not a number".</b> Without it, statsmodels would multiply your category codes (1,2,3) as if plan 3 were "three times" plan 1.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Fitting models with formulas', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/example_formulas.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Reading <code>summary()</code> — the interview reads it line by line:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>coef       effect: unit change in x → coef change in y, HOLDING OTHER x FIXED\nstd err    uncertainty of that estimate\nt, P&gt;|t|   significance; P&gt;|t| &lt; 0.05 → "distinguishable from 0"\n[0.025 0.975]  95% confidence interval for the coef\nR-squared / Adj. R-squared   variance explained (Adj penalises extra predictors)\nF-statistic (Prob)           are the predictors JOINTLY useful?\nLogit: exp(coef) = ODDS RATIO   (coef in log-odds)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>"Does price drive churn, controlling for tenure and plan?" → <code>smf.logit(\'churn ~ price + tenure + C(plan)\', df).fit()</code>. If price coef = 0.04 (p&lt;0.01), then <code>exp(0.04) ≈ 1.04</code>: each extra dollar multiplies the churn odds by ~1.04, tenure and plan held constant.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 OLS example', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/regression.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Assumptions and diagnostics (this is what separates a stats hire from a "ran .fit()" hire):</p>' +
    '<pre><code>linearity            residual-vs-fitted plot should be a flat cloud\nindependence         no autocorrelation (Durbin-Watson ≈ 2); time series → use tsa models\nhomoscedasticity     constant residual spread; test: Breusch-Pagan / White. Fix: cov_type=\'HC3\'\nnormal residuals     QQ-plot; matters mainly for small-sample inference\nno multicollinearity  VIF &gt; 5–10 → coefficients unstable (not a prediction problem)\ninfluential points    Cook\'s distance, leverage\n\nstatsmodels is NOT a prediction library: no pipelines, no CV, no regularisation by default\n(fit_regularized exists). For prediction + tuning, use sklearn.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Regression diagnostics', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/diagnostic.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: sklearn LinearRegression vs statsmodels OLS?\nA: sklearn = prediction, pipelines, CV, regularisation, deployment. statsmodels = inference:\n   p-values, CIs, diagnostics, the summary table. Different jobs; often use both.\n\nQ: Interpret a coefficient of -2.5 on "discount_pct" in an OLS predicting units_sold... wait, that is\n   positive normally — interpret +2.5.\nA: Each 1-unit increase in discount_pct is associated with 2.5 more units sold, holding all other\n   predictors fixed. For logit, you would exponentiate to get an odds ratio.\n\nQ: R^2 vs adjusted R^2?\nA: R^2 never decreases when you add predictors. Adjusted R^2 penalises added predictors, so it can\n   drop if the new variable does not help — use it to compare models with different #features.\n\nQ: What is multicollinearity, how do you detect it, why care?\nA: Predictors linearly related to each other. Detect with VIF (or a correlation matrix / condition\n   number). It inflates coefficient standard errors → unstable, uninterpretable coefficients.\n   Prediction accuracy is usually fine.\n\nQ: Residuals fan out as fitted values grow. What is it and one fix?\nA: Heteroscedasticity. Fix: robust (HC3) standard errors, or model log(y), or weighted least squares.\n\nQ: Why sm.add_constant(X)?\nA: OLS has no intercept unless you include a column of 1s; without it the line is forced through 0.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 statsmodels vs sklearn', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/gettingstarted.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'You need p-values and confidence intervals on each predictor to report which factors significantly affect a KPI. Which tool?', opts: ['sklearn LinearRegression', 'statsmodels OLS (or smf.ols) and read summary()', 'XGBoost feature_importances_', 'KMeans'], ok: 1, why: 'sklearn does not expose inferential statistics. statsmodels gives coefficients with standard errors, t-stats, p-values, CIs and diagnostics — exactly what "which factors significantly affect" requires.' },
    { q: 'In a logistic regression, a coefficient is 0.69 (p = 0.004) for a binary feature "is_promo". Interpretation?', opts: ['Promo increases the probability by 69%', 'Being in promo multiplies the ODDS of the positive outcome by exp(0.69) ≈ 2, holding other predictors fixed', 'Promo has no effect', 'The probability doubles'], ok: 1, why: 'Logit coefficients are in log-odds. exp(coef) is the odds ratio: exp(0.69) ≈ 2, so promo roughly doubles the odds (not the probability) of the event, ceteris paribus.' },
    { q: 'Adding a highly correlated new predictor barely changes R² but the standard errors of related coefficients blow up. What is happening?', opts: ['Overfitting to the test set', 'Multicollinearity — check VIF; coefficients become unstable even though fit is fine', 'Heteroscedasticity', 'The intercept is missing'], ok: 1, why: 'Correlated predictors carry overlapping information, so the model cannot cleanly attribute effect to each — standard errors inflate. VIF quantifies it. Prediction quality is typically unaffected.' } ] },

/* ============================ 6. SCIKIT-LEARN I ============================ */
{ num: 6, emoji: '⚙️', title: 'scikit-learn — Estimators, Pipelines, Preprocessing', layer: 'Classic ML',
  tagline: 'One API — fit / predict / transform — and one object (the Pipeline) that kills data leakage.',
  apps: ['almost every tabular ML baseline', 'preprocessing for XGBoost / PyTorch too', 'the model you actually deploy'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Every scikit-learn object speaks the same four words: <code>.fit(X, y)</code> learns, <code>.predict(X)</code> applies, <code>.transform(X)</code> changes data, <code>.fit_transform(X)</code> does both. Learn the grammar once; 200 models and transformers obey it.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nclf = LogisticRegression()\nclf.fit(X_train, y_train)\nclf.predict(X_test)\nclf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]        # probability of the positive class</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Every appliance uses the same three-pin plug.</b> Once you know the socket, you can swap the kettle for the toaster without rewiring the kitchen.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 scikit-learn docs', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/', 'o'], ['📘 Part 1: build the estimators from scratch', '../learn/#ch7', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Three kinds of object: <b>estimators</b> (models), <b>transformers</b> (scalers, encoders, PCA), and <b>meta-estimators</b> that wrap others (<code>Pipeline</code>, <code>GridSearchCV</code>).</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\npipe = Pipeline([\n    (\'scale\', StandardScaler()),\n    (\'clf\',   LogisticRegression()),\n])\npipe.fit(X_train, y_train)      # one call fits BOTH steps\npipe.predict(X_test)            # scales, then predicts</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>An assembly line.</b> Each station transforms the part; the last station makes the decision. The whole line has one on-switch (<code>.fit</code>) and one output chute (<code>.predict</code>).</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Pipeline', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/compose.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The preprocessing you reach for:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>StandardScaler / MinMaxScaler / RobustScaler      # numeric scaling\nOneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\')            # nominal categoricals\nOrdinalEncoder                                    # ordered categoricals\nSimpleImputer(strategy=\'median\')                   # fill missing\nColumnTransformer(...)                             # different treatment per column\n\nfrom sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\npre = ColumnTransformer([\n  (\'num\', Pipeline([(\'imp\', SimpleImputer(strategy=\'median\')), (\'sc\', StandardScaler())]), num_cols),\n  (\'cat\', OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\'), cat_cols),\n])\nfull = Pipeline([(\'pre\', pre), (\'clf\', LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000))])</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>A mixed dataframe becomes one deployable object: <code>full.fit(X_train, y_train)</code>. At serving time you pass raw rows and it imputes, scales, encodes and predicts — no separate preprocessing script to drift.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 ColumnTransformer', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.compose.ColumnTransformer.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>The data-leakage rule.</b> Any statistic learned from data (a mean, a scale, an encoding, an imputation value) must be learned from the <b>training fold only</b>. Fit it on all the data before splitting and test information bleeds into training.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>WRONG:  X = StandardScaler().fit_transform(X);  X_tr, X_te = split(X)   # leak\nRIGHT:  put the scaler IN the Pipeline; cross_val_score refits it inside every fold\n\nother essentials:\n• transform() on test, never fit()/fit_transform()\n• pipe.set_output(transform=\'pandas\') to keep column names\n• custom transformer: subclass BaseEstimator, TransformerMixin (fit returns self, implement transform)\n• get_feature_names_out() to trace one-hot columns</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>An open-book exam where the book contains the answer key.</b> If the scaler saw the test set\'s mean, the model already "knows" something about the test distribution. Your reported score is optimistic and will not hold in production.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Common pitfalls: data leakage', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Why use a Pipeline instead of transforming, then fitting a model?\nA: (1) No leakage — transformers refit inside each CV fold. (2) One object to cross-validate,\n   grid-search, pickle and deploy. (3) No train/serve skew — the exact preprocessing ships with the model.\n\nQ: Where exactly does leakage happen in preprocessing?\nA: Any fit on data that includes rows you will later evaluate on: scaling, imputation, target/\n   frequency encoding, feature selection, SMOTE, or PCA done before the split (or outside the CV loop).\n\nQ: StandardScaler vs MinMaxScaler vs RobustScaler?\nA: Standard: mean 0 / std 1, good default for linear models & NNs. MinMax: squashes to [0,1], for\n   bounded inputs / images. Robust: uses median & IQR, resistant to outliers.\n\nQ: fit vs transform vs fit_transform — which do you call on the test set?\nA: transform only. fit / fit_transform would re-learn parameters from test data.\n\nQ: A category appears at prediction time that was never in training. How do you avoid a crash?\nA: OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\') (encodes it as all-zeros), or a target encoder with a\n   prior/fallback.\n\nQ: Do you scale before or after train/test split?\nA: Split first, then fit the scaler on train only (in practice: put it in a Pipeline).</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Preprocessing data', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/preprocessing.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What is the main reason to wrap preprocessing + model in a single sklearn Pipeline?', opts: ['It runs faster', 'It prevents data leakage (transformers refit per CV fold) and ships preprocessing with the model, avoiding train/serve skew', 'It reduces the number of features', 'It is required for predict_proba'], ok: 1, why: 'A Pipeline makes cross-validation honest (each fold refits transformers on its own training rows) and turns the whole preprocess+model into one deployable object, eliminating a separate preprocessing script that can drift.' },
    { q: 'Which sequence is correct for scaling features?', opts: ['fit_transform on the full dataset, then train_test_split', 'train_test_split, then fit the scaler on X_train and transform both X_train and X_test', 'fit the scaler on X_test', 'scale only the target y'], ok: 1, why: 'The scaler must learn its mean/std from training data only. Fitting on the full dataset (or on test) leaks test-distribution information and inflates your validation score.' },
    { q: 'At inference a new category "platinum" appears in "tier", unseen in training. With OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore"), what happens?', opts: ['It raises a ValueError', 'The row is dropped', 'All tier one-hot columns are 0 for that row and prediction proceeds', 'It creates a new column automatically'], ok: 2, why: 'handle_unknown="ignore" encodes unseen categories as an all-zero vector across the known dummy columns, so the pipeline keeps working instead of crashing.' } ] },

/* ============================ 7. SCIKIT-LEARN II ============================ */
{ num: 7, emoji: '🎯', title: 'scikit-learn — Model Selection, Metrics & Imbalance', layer: 'Classic ML',
  tagline: 'Cross-validation that does not lie, the right metric for the problem, and what to do about rare classes.',
  apps: ['every "is my model actually good?" question', 'fraud / churn / disease — rare positives', 'model bake-offs'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>You cannot grade a student on the exact questions they studied. Hold out data the model never saw; better, do it many times and average.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=0)\nscores = cross_val_score(pipe, X_tr, y_tr, cv=5, scoring=\'roc_auc\')\nscores.mean(), scores.std()</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Five mock exams on fresh questions, then average the grade.</b> One lucky (or unlucky) split tells you little; the spread across folds tells you how stable the model is.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Cross-validation', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Pick the splitter for the data, or you leak:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>KFold              generic\nStratifiedKFold    keeps class ratios per fold — DEFAULT for classification\nGroupKFold         no group (e.g. same user/patient) appears in both train and val\nTimeSeriesSplit    train on past, validate on future — NEVER shuffle time series\n</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Shuffling a deck vs dealing a diary in order.</b> For a diary (time series), shuffling lets you "revise for Tuesday using Wednesday" — guaranteed leakage. Deal it in order.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Cross-validation iterators', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html#cross-validation-iterators', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The metric IS the problem definition:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>classification:\n  accuracy         useless under imbalance\n  precision        of predicted positives, how many are right  (cost of false alarms)\n  recall           of actual positives, how many we caught      (cost of misses)\n  F1               harmonic mean of the two\n  ROC-AUC          rank quality across all thresholds; optimistic under heavy imbalance\n  PR-AUC           preferred when positives are rare\nregression: MAE (robust), RMSE (penalises big errors), R^2, MAPE (breaks near 0)\n\nfrom sklearn.metrics import classification_report, confusion_matrix\nproba = clf.predict_proba(X_te)[:, 1]\npred  = (proba &gt;= 0.3).astype(int)     # tune the threshold for YOUR precision/recall need</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Fraud: 0.5% positive. Accuracy of "predict not-fraud" is 99.5% and catches nothing. Optimise <b>PR-AUC</b> or "recall at ≥ 90% precision", and choose the threshold from the precision-recall curve, not 0.5.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Metrics and scoring', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Imbalanced data — the fixes, in order of "try first":</p>' +
    '<pre><code>1. class_weight=\'balanced\'  (LogReg, SVM, RandomForest) / scale_pos_weight (XGB/LGBM)\n2. threshold tuning on predict_proba\n3. resample INSIDE the pipeline/fold: imblearn.pipeline.Pipeline + SMOTE / RandomUnderSampler\n   — SMOTE before the split = leakage (synthetic points near test rows)\n4. anomaly-detection framing if positives are truly rare & unlabelled-ish\n\ncalibration: CalibratedClassifierCV + reliability curve — needed if you use the probabilities\ncross_validate(return_train_score=True) → gap between train & val = overfitting\nnested CV for an honest score of a TUNED model</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>SMOTE before splitting is photocopying the exam and leaving a copy in the study room.</b> The model meets near-duplicates of test cases during training and looks brilliant — until production.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 imbalanced-learn', 'https://imbalanced-learn.org/stable/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Model is 97% accurate but useless. Why?\nA: Imbalance. If 97% of rows are the negative class, predicting "always negative" scores 97% and\n   catches zero positives. Use precision/recall/F1/PR-AUC and a tuned threshold.\n\nQ: Precision vs recall — give a case where each matters more.\nA: Spam filter → precision (a real email in spam is bad). Cancer screening → recall (a missed case\n   is bad). You trade them via the threshold.\n\nQ: ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC?\nA: ROC-AUC = ranking quality over all thresholds, uses TPR vs FPR; stays high even with rare positives.\n   PR-AUC uses precision vs recall and is far more sensitive when the positive class is rare — prefer it there.\n\nQ: How do you cross-validate a time series?\nA: TimeSeriesSplit / expanding or rolling window; train only on the past, optionally leave a gap;\n   never shuffle; report the mean over folds.\n\nQ: You applied SMOTE then split. What is wrong?\nA: Leakage. Synthetic samples are interpolations of real points, including ones that end up in test.\n   Resample only within the training fold (imblearn Pipeline inside CV).\n\nQ: Name 4 ways data leakage sneaks in.\nA: Preprocessing fit before split; target-derived features; duplicate rows across splits; time\n   leakage (future info); tuning on the test set; grouped entities split across folds.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Tuning hyperparameters', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/grid_search.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A churn model reports 92% ROC-AUC but the business says it "misses most churners". Best next check?', opts: ['Retrain with more trees', 'Look at recall / PR-AUC and the confusion matrix at the operating threshold — ROC-AUC can look good while recall at 0.5 is low under imbalance', 'Switch to accuracy', 'Add more features blindly'], ok: 1, why: 'ROC-AUC measures ranking, not the decisions made at a fixed threshold. Under imbalance, recall at 0.5 can be poor. Inspect PR-AUC, recall, and tune the threshold to the business cost.' },
    { q: 'For validating a demand-forecasting model on daily data, which splitter?', opts: ['KFold(shuffle=True)', 'StratifiedKFold', 'TimeSeriesSplit (or an expanding-window scheme)', 'LeaveOneOut'], ok: 2, why: 'Time series must be split chronologically so the model only ever trains on the past. Shuffled KFold leaks future information into training and massively overstates accuracy.' },
    { q: 'You want to oversample the minority class with SMOTE. Where must it go?', opts: ['On the whole dataset before train_test_split', 'Inside the cross-validation pipeline so it only touches the training fold', 'On the test set', 'After computing metrics'], ok: 1, why: 'SMOTE creates synthetic points by interpolating real ones. Doing it before the split can place near-duplicates of test rows into training. Use an imblearn Pipeline so resampling happens only within each training fold.' } ] },

/* ============================ 8. GRADIENT BOOSTING ============================ */
{ num: 8, emoji: '🌳', title: 'XGBoost, LightGBM & CatBoost', layer: 'Classic ML',
  tagline: 'The default winner on tabular data: hundreds of shallow trees, each fixing the last one\'s mistakes.',
  apps: ['Kaggle tabular leaderboards', 'credit scoring / risk', 'ranking, CTR, demand forecasting on features'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p><b>Gradient boosting</b> trains trees one after another; each new tree is fit to the <i>errors</i> (residuals / gradients) of the ensemble so far. Add them up and a pile of weak trees becomes a strong model.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A relay of proof-readers.</b> The first reader catches the obvious typos. The second only reads what the first left wrong. The tenth is fixing subtle things. Stack their corrections and the document is clean.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>from xgboost import XGBClassifier\nm = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=600, learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=5,\n                  subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8)\nm.fit(X_tr, y_tr, eval_set=[(X_val, y_val)], early_stopping_rounds=50)</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 XGBoost', 'https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/', 'o'], ['📘 Part 1: boosting-style residual fitting', '../learn/#ch1', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Same idea, three implementations:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>XGBoost   the original; level-wise trees; very robust; huge ecosystem\nLightGBM  leaf-wise growth + histogram binning → fastest, best on large data,\n          but can overfit small data (raise min_child_samples, lower num_leaves)\nCatBoost  native categorical handling + ordered boosting → strongest out-of-the-box,\n          great when you have many categorical columns; less tuning needed\nAll expose a scikit-learn API (fit/predict), so they drop into a Pipeline.</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Same engine, different tuning.</b> LightGBM is the drag racer (fastest on a clean track / big data). CatBoost is the car that starts in any weather (messy categoricals, minimal setup). XGBoost is the reliable all-rounder.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 LightGBM', 'https://lightgbm.readthedocs.io/', 'o'], ['📖 CatBoost', 'https://catboost.ai/en/docs/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The knobs that actually move the needle:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>learning_rate  + n_estimators   small LR (0.01-0.05) + many trees + EARLY STOPPING\nmax_depth / num_leaves          model capacity; leaf-wise: keep num_leaves under 2^max_depth\nsubsample / colsample_bytree    row/column sampling per tree = regularisation\nmin_child_weight / min_child_samples   minimum data per leaf = anti-overfit\nreg_alpha (L1) / reg_lambda (L2)       weight penalties\nscale_pos_weight / class_weights       imbalance</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Tabular problem, 200k rows: LightGBM, <code>learning_rate=0.02</code>, <code>num_leaves=63</code>, row/col subsample 0.8, early stopping on a held-out validation fold. Then SHAP (ch.9) to explain the drivers.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 LightGBM parameter tuning', 'https://lightgbm.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Parameters-Tuning.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The traps:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• EARLY STOPPING needs a validation set that is NOT your test set — else you tune on test\n• LightGBM overfits tiny datasets (leaf-wise is greedy) → min_child_samples up, num_leaves down, more bagging\n• DO NOT one-hot high-cardinality categoricals for LGBM/CatBoost — pass categorical_feature / cat_features\n• default n_estimators=100 + small LR = undertrained; let early stopping decide\n• missing values handled natively — no imputation needed (they learn a default direction)\n• trees are scale-invariant — no need to StandardScaler\n• importance types (gain / split / cover) disagree and are biased toward high-cardinality features\n  → use SHAP or permutation importance instead</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Leaf-wise growth is a student who keeps drilling the one topic that gains the most marks.</b> Brilliant on a big syllabus, disastrous on a 10-question quiz — it memorises. Constrain it on small data.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 XGBoost parameters', 'https://xgboost.readthedocs.io/en/stable/parameter.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Bagging vs boosting?\nA: Bagging trains many models INDEPENDENTLY on bootstrap samples and averages (reduces variance,\n   e.g. Random Forest). Boosting trains models SEQUENTIALLY, each on the previous errors (reduces bias).\n\nQ: Random Forest vs Gradient Boosting — tradeoffs?\nA: RF: parallel, few knobs, hard to overfit, strong baseline. GBM: sequential, needs tuning\n   (LR, depth, early stopping), lower bias, usually wins on tabular but is more fragile.\n\nQ: Relationship between learning_rate and n_estimators?\nA: Inverse. Lower LR = each tree contributes less = need more trees, but generalises better.\n   Standard recipe: small LR + large n_estimators + early stopping.\n\nQ: How does XGBoost handle missing values?\nA: At each split it learns a default direction (left/right) for missing values from the training data;\n   no imputation required.\n\nQ: Why can LightGBM be faster than XGBoost?\nA: Histogram-based split finding (bin features into ~255 buckets) + leaf-wise growth + efficient\n   handling of sparse/categorical data.\n\nQ: Your GBM overfits. Three fixes.\nA: Lower learning_rate + early stopping; reduce max_depth/num_leaves; increase subsample/\n   colsample and min_child_samples; add reg_lambda; get more data.\n\nQ: Built-in feature importance is misleading — why, and the alternative?\nA: Split-count/gain importance is biased toward high-cardinality and correlated features and is\n   global-only. Use SHAP (consistent, local + global) or permutation importance.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Elements of Statistical Learning (boosting)', 'https://hastie.su.domains/ElemStatLearn/', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'What is each new tree in gradient boosting trained to predict?', opts: ['The original target from scratch', 'The residual error / negative gradient of the loss left by the ensemble so far', 'A random subset of the target', 'The average of all previous predictions'], ok: 1, why: 'Boosting is additive and sequential: tree k is fit to the part of the target the first k-1 trees got wrong (the negative gradient of the loss), then added with a small learning-rate weight.' },
    { q: 'You lower learning_rate from 0.1 to 0.01. What else should change?', opts: ['Decrease n_estimators', 'Increase n_estimators substantially (and rely on early stopping to pick the count)', 'Nothing', 'Increase max_depth to 20'], ok: 1, why: 'Each tree now contributes less, so you need many more trees to reach the same fit. The standard approach is a small learning rate with a large n_estimators cap and early stopping on a validation set.' },
    { q: 'Why prefer SHAP or permutation importance over XGBoost\'s default feature_importances_?', opts: ['They are faster', 'Default gain/split importance is biased toward high-cardinality and correlated features and is global-only; SHAP/permutation are more consistent and give per-row explanations', 'They always agree with the default', 'The default is deprecated'], ok: 1, why: 'Impurity/gain-based importance inflates features that offer many split points and cannot explain individual predictions. SHAP has consistency guarantees and works locally and globally; permutation importance is model-agnostic.' } ] },

/* ============================ 9. EXPLAINABILITY & TUNING ============================ */
{ num: 9, emoji: '🔍', title: 'SHAP, LIME & Hyperparameter Tuning', layer: 'Classic ML',
  tagline: 'Explain a single prediction to a stakeholder, and search hyperparameters without fooling yourself.',
  apps: ['"why was this loan denied?" (regulated ML)', 'model debugging & trust', 'squeezing the last 2% with Optuna'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>The model says "denied". <b>SHAP</b> turns that into an itemised bill: <code>+120 low income, +40 short history, −15 no past defaults</code> — every feature\'s contribution to <i>this one</i> prediction, and they sum to the output minus a baseline.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import shap\nexplainer = shap.TreeExplainer(model)      # fast + exact for tree models\nsv = explainer(X)\nshap.plots.beeswarm(sv)                     # global: which features matter, and which direction\nshap.plots.waterfall(sv[0])                 # local: why row 0 got its prediction</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>An itemised restaurant bill, not just the total.</b> "Why is it $80?" — SHAP lists every dish and its price, and they add up to $80.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 SHAP docs', 'https://shap.readthedocs.io/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>SHAP</b> = Shapley values from cooperative game theory: fairly divide the "payout" (prediction − expected value) among "players" (features), averaged over every order of adding them. <b>LIME</b> = fit a tiny interpretable model in the local neighbourhood of one point.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>shap.TreeExplainer   trees (XGB/LGBM/RF) — fast, exact\nshap.LinearExplainer linear models\nshap.KernelExplainer any model — model-agnostic but slow (sampling)\nshap.Explainer       auto-picks\n</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>SHAP is a full audited split of a group bill; LIME is a quick back-of-napkin sketch of "around here, spice level pushes the price up".</b> SHAP is consistent and additive; LIME is faster but its explanation can wobble if you re-run it.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 LIME', 'https://github.com/marcotcr/lime', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Reading the plots, and a model-agnostic sanity check:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>beeswarm / summary   features ranked by mean|SHAP|; colour = feature value → see direction\ndependence plot      SHAP of feature f vs its value → shape + interaction with another feature\nwaterfall / force    one prediction decomposed into +/- contributions\n\nfrom sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\nr = permutation_importance(model, X_val, y_val, n_repeats=10, scoring=\'roc_auc\')\n# shuffle a column, measure the score drop → model-agnostic, honest about correlated features (as a pair)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Churn model: beeswarm shows <code>tenure_months</code> and <code>support_tickets</code> dominate. The dependence plot for tenure shows risk spikes sharply below 3 months → product team targets a 90-day onboarding campaign.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Interpretable ML (Molnar)', 'https://christophm.github.io/interpretable-ml-book/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Hyperparameter optimisation — and how not to lie to yourself:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>GridSearchCV        exhaustive over a grid — dies combinatorially past ~3 params\nRandomizedSearchCV  random draws — usually more score per compute-minute\nOptuna              Bayesian (TPE) + prunes hopeless trials early; define search space as a function\n\nimport optuna\ndef objective(trial):\n    params = dict(\n        learning_rate=trial.suggest_float(\'lr\', 1e-3, 0.3, log=True),\n        num_leaves=trial.suggest_int(\'nl\', 15, 255),\n    )\n    return cross_val_score(LGBMClassifier(**params), X_tr, y_tr, cv=5, scoring=\'roc_auc\').mean()\nstudy = optuna.create_study(direction=\'maximize\'); study.optimize(objective, n_trials=50)\n\nGOTCHAS: tune on CV or a val split, NEVER the test set · log-scale LR & regularisation ·\nfix seeds · too many trials overfits the validation set · report the FINAL model on untouched test data</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Optuna', 'https://optuna.org/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Explain a model prediction to a non-technical stakeholder.\nA: SHAP local (waterfall/force) → "the model expected ~12% churn; this customer is at 41% mostly\n   because tenure is 1 month (+22pp) and they opened 4 tickets (+9pp)."\n\nQ: SHAP vs permutation importance vs built-in importance?\nA: Built-in: fast, global, biased (cardinality/correlation), tree-only meaning. Permutation:\n   model-agnostic, measures real score impact, but splits credit oddly among correlated features.\n   SHAP: local + global, additive, consistency guarantees; TreeExplainer is cheap for trees.\n\nQ: What is a Shapley value, intuitively?\nA: A feature\'s average marginal contribution to the prediction across all possible orders in which\n   features could be added to the model. It is the unique fair attribution satisfying efficiency,\n   symmetry, dummy and additivity.\n\nQ: GridSearch vs RandomSearch vs Bayesian — when each?\nA: Grid: few params, small ranges. Random: many params / limited budget (Bergstra & Bengio:\n   random beats grid per trial). Bayesian (Optuna): expensive objective, want sample-efficiency + pruning.\n\nQ: Val score is great, test score is bad, after 200 tuning trials. What happened?\nA: You overfit the validation set (multiple-comparisons on the CV score) — or there is leakage.\n   Fixes: fewer trials, nested CV, a truly held-out final test set, simpler search space.\n\nQ: Global vs local explanation?\nA: Global = behaviour across the dataset (which features matter overall). Local = why THIS row got\n   THIS prediction. Beeswarm is global; waterfall is local.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Random Search for Hyper-Parameter Optimization', 'https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v13/bergstra12a.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A regulator asks why a specific applicant was rejected. Which tool answers that best?', opts: ['Global feature_importances_ bar chart', 'A SHAP local explanation (waterfall/force plot) for that applicant\'s row', 'The confusion matrix', 'The ROC curve'], ok: 1, why: 'Global importance says which features matter on average, not why this person was rejected. A SHAP local explanation decomposes that single prediction into per-feature contributions that sum to the score.' },
    { q: 'You ran Optuna for 300 trials; CV score climbed steadily but the untouched test score is much lower. Most likely cause?', opts: ['Optuna is buggy', 'Overfitting the validation signal across many trials (a multiple-comparisons effect) and/or leakage', 'Not enough trials', 'You should have used GridSearch'], ok: 1, why: 'Searching hard against a CV score eventually fits its noise. Use fewer trials, nested CV, or a final held-out test set you never optimise against; also check for leakage in preprocessing.' },
    { q: 'Why can RandomizedSearchCV beat GridSearchCV for the same compute budget?', opts: ['It always finds the global optimum', 'With many hyperparameters, random sampling explores each important dimension at more distinct values instead of wasting trials on a coarse full grid', 'It skips cross-validation', 'It only tunes the learning rate'], ok: 1, why: 'Grid search spends trials on every combination including unimportant dimensions. Random search, per Bergstra & Bengio, covers the influential parameters more finely for the same number of evaluations.' } ] },

/* ============================ 10. PYTORCH ============================ */
{ num: 10, emoji: '🔥', title: 'PyTorch', layer: 'Deep Learning',
  tagline: 'NumPy on a GPU that remembers how every number was computed, so it can hand you the gradients.',
  apps: ['most deep-learning research + many products', 'Hugging Face models run on it', 'custom architectures, RL, GNNs'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>A <b>tensor</b> is an ndarray that can live on a GPU and, if you ask, records the operations done to it so <code>.backward()</code> can compute gradients automatically.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import torch\nx = torch.tensor(2.0, requires_grad=True)\ny = x ** 3 + 2 * x\ny.backward()\nx.grad            # tensor(14.)  = 3x^2 + 2 at x=2\nx.to(\'cuda\')       # move to GPU</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A calculator that keeps the receipt.</b> Every operation is logged, so at the end you can walk the receipt backwards and see how much each input affected the total (the gradient).</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 PyTorch docs', 'https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/', 'o'], ['📘 Part 1: build autograd from scratch', '../learn/#ch1', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Four pieces, always the same:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>1. Tensors      data + params (requires_grad)\n2. autograd     builds a graph on the fly; loss.backward() fills .grad on every leaf\n3. nn.Module    layers with a forward(); model.parameters() yields the tensors to train\n4. torch.optim  Adam/SGD; opt.step() applies the update, opt.zero_grad() clears grads\n\nclass Net(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(20, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, 1))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Lego.</b> Tensors are bricks, <code>nn.Module</code>s are pre-built sub-assemblies, the training loop is the instruction booklet you follow every time.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Learn the Basics', 'https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The training loop you write for every model:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>model.to(device)\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nloss_fn = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\nfor epoch in range(EPOCHS):\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=64, shuffle=True):\n        xb, yb = xb.to(device), yb.to(device)\n        opt.zero_grad()\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n        loss.backward()\n        opt.step()\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        val_loss = evaluate(model, val_loader)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Tabular MLP or fine-tune: wrap your arrays in a <code>Dataset</code> (returns <code>(x, y)</code>), let <code>DataLoader</code> batch + shuffle, move each batch to the device. That is 90% of "training in PyTorch".</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Training a classifier', 'https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/blitz/cifar10_tutorial.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The bugs everyone hits at least once:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• forgot opt.zero_grad()   → grads ACCUMULATE across steps → training explodes / diverges\n• forgot model.eval()      → dropout & batchnorm still in "train" mode at inference → wrong outputs\n• forgot torch.no_grad()   in eval → graph is kept → OOM on the validation loop\n• CPU tensor + GPU model   → "Expected all tensors on the same device"\n• logging loss (not loss.item()) → keeps the whole graph alive → memory leak\n• LR too high → NaN loss; also check input scaling and log(0)\n• .numpy() on a grad tensor → call .detach().cpu().numpy()\n\nscaling up: torch.cuda.amp (mixed precision), DataLoader(num_workers=4, pin_memory=True),\nnn.utils.clip_grad_norm_, gradient accumulation, torch.compile()</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b><code>zero_grad()</code> is wiping the whiteboard between problems.</b> Skip it and problem 2\'s working is scribbled on top of problem 1\'s — the answer is nonsense.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Common mistakes / recipe', 'https://pytorch.org/tutorials/recipes/recipes/tuning_guide.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: What does loss.backward() do?\nA: Traverses the autograd graph from loss back to the leaves, applying the chain rule, and\n   accumulates d(loss)/d(param) into each param\'s .grad.\n\nQ: Why optimizer.zero_grad() every step?\nA: .grad accumulates by default (useful for grad accumulation). Without clearing, step k uses\n   the sum of gradients from steps 1..k.\n\nQ: model.train() vs model.eval() — what actually changes?\nA: Dropout is disabled in eval; BatchNorm uses its running mean/var instead of batch stats.\n   Layers check self.training.\n\nQ: Why torch.no_grad() in the eval loop?\nA: Stops autograd from building the graph → less memory, faster; you are not going to call backward.\n\nQ: Loss is NaN. Checklist.\nA: LR too high; unnormalised / huge inputs; log(0) or divide-by-0 in the loss; exploding gradients\n   (clip); bad target values; fp16 overflow (use bf16 / loss scaling).\n\nQ: Relationship between batch size and learning rate?\nA: Larger batches give lower-variance gradients → you can (roughly linearly, up to a point) scale\n   the LR up; often add warmup.\n\nQ: "Expected all tensors to be on the same device" — fix?\nA: Move both model and every input batch (and the loss target) to the same device with .to(device).\n\nQ: Why .detach() before .numpy()?\nA: A tensor that requires grad is attached to the graph; NumPy conversion needs a plain tensor,\n   so detach (and .cpu() if on GPU) first.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Autograd mechanics', 'https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/autograd.html', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'You remove optimizer.zero_grad() from your loop. What happens?', opts: ['Nothing changes', 'Gradients accumulate across iterations, so each update uses a growing sum of past gradients and training destabilises', 'The model trains faster', 'The loss becomes exactly zero'], ok: 1, why: 'PyTorch accumulates .grad by design. Without zeroing, step k applies the sum of gradients from steps 1..k, which usually blows up or stalls training.' },
    { q: 'At inference your model gives inconsistent outputs for the same input. Most likely omission?', opts: ['Forgot loss.backward()', 'Forgot model.eval(), so Dropout is still randomly zeroing activations and BatchNorm uses batch stats', 'Wrong learning rate', 'Missing optimizer'], ok: 1, why: 'model.eval() switches Dropout off and makes BatchNorm use running statistics. In train mode, Dropout randomness makes each forward pass different.' },
    { q: 'Your validation loop OOMs even though the model fits in training. Best fix?', opts: ['Reduce the model size', 'Wrap the eval forward pass in torch.no_grad() (and use loss.item() for logging)', 'Increase batch size', 'Call backward() in eval'], ok: 1, why: 'Without no_grad(), autograd keeps the computation graph for every eval batch, consuming memory. torch.no_grad() disables graph construction; logging the tensor itself also pins the graph, so use .item().' } ] },

/* ============================ 11. TENSORFLOW / KERAS ============================ */
{ num: 11, emoji: '🧠', title: 'TensorFlow & Keras', layer: 'Deep Learning',
  tagline: 'The other major framework. Keras gives you compile + fit + callbacks instead of a hand-written loop.',
  apps: ['production DL at large orgs', 'mobile/edge (TFLite) & TF Serving', 'quick prototypes via keras.fit'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p><b>TensorFlow</b> is the numeric/graph engine; <b>Keras</b> is its high-level API (now also multi-backend in Keras 3). Where PyTorch hands you the training loop, Keras hands you <code>model.compile()</code> then <code>model.fit()</code>.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import keras\nmodel = keras.Sequential([\n    keras.layers.Dense(64, activation=\'relu\'),\n    keras.layers.Dense(1, activation=\'sigmoid\'),\n])\nmodel.compile(optimizer=\'adam\', loss=\'binary_crossentropy\', metrics=[\'AUC\'])\nmodel.fit(X_tr, y_tr, validation_data=(X_val, y_val), epochs=20)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>PyTorch is a manual car; Keras is an automatic.</b> Same destinations; the automatic hides the clutch (<code>fit</code>) but you can still drop into manual (<code>train_step</code> subclassing) when you need it.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Keras docs', 'https://keras.io/', 'o'], ['📖 TensorFlow', 'https://www.tensorflow.org/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Three ways to define a model:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>Sequential      a linear stack of layers — 90% of cases\nFunctional API  a graph: multi-input/output, skip/residual connections, shared layers\n  inp = keras.Input((20,)); x = layers.Dense(64, activation=\'relu\')(inp)\n  out = layers.Dense(1)(x); model = keras.Model(inp, out)\nSubclassing     class MyModel(keras.Model): define __init__ + call() — full control, PyTorch-like</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Sequential = a train (cars in one line). Functional = a rail network with junctions. Subclassing = you drive the locomotive.</b></p></div>',
    try: [['📖 The Functional API', 'https://keras.io/guides/functional_api/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The features you actually rely on:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>callbacks = [\n  keras.callbacks.EarlyStopping(patience=5, restore_best_weights=True),\n  keras.callbacks.ReduceLROnPlateau(factor=0.5, patience=3),\n  keras.callbacks.ModelCheckpoint(\'best.keras\', save_best_only=True),\n]\nds = (tf.data.Dataset.from_tensor_slices((X, y))\n        .shuffle(10_000).batch(64).prefetch(tf.data.AUTOTUNE))\nmodel.fit(ds, epochs=50, callbacks=callbacks)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Image classifier: <code>keras.utils.image_dataset_from_directory</code> → augmentation layers → a frozen <code>keras.applications</code> backbone + new head → <code>fit</code> → unfreeze top layers → <code>fit</code> again at a low LR. Transfer learning in ~30 lines.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 tf.data pipelines', 'https://www.tensorflow.org/guide/data_performance', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Gotchas:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• sparse_categorical_crossentropy (integer labels) vs categorical_crossentropy (one-hot) — mixing them silently breaks training\n• forgetting .prefetch()/.cache() → GPU starves waiting on the input pipeline\n• BatchNormalization behaves differently train vs inference (handled by fit/predict, matters on export)\n• saving: model.save(\'m.keras\') (full) vs SavedModel dir (serving) vs save_weights (weights only)\n• shape errors from a missing batch dimension; expand_dims\n• TF1 graph-mode habits — TF2 is eager by default; @tf.function to compile hot paths\n• Keras 3 runs on TF / JAX / PyTorch backends — pick via KERAS_BACKEND</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Save and load models', 'https://keras.io/guides/serialization_and_saving/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: PyTorch vs TensorFlow/Keras — pick one and defend it.\nA: PyTorch: pythonic, easy debugging, dominant in research, dynamic graphs. TF/Keras: mature\n   serving (TF Serving), mobile/edge (TFLite), keras.fit ergonomics, TPU support. Today they\n   converge and Keras 3 is multi-backend, so the honest answer is "either; here is my project\'s\n   deployment constraint...".\n\nQ: Sequential vs Functional API — when do you NEED Functional?\nA: Multiple inputs or outputs, shared layers, residual/skip connections, or any non-linear\n   topology. Sequential can only express a single straight stack.\n\nQ: What does EarlyStopping(restore_best_weights=True) do?\nA: Stops training when the monitored metric has not improved for `patience` epochs AND rolls the\n   weights back to the best epoch, not the last.\n\nQ: categorical vs sparse_categorical crossentropy?\nA: categorical expects one-hot targets; sparse expects integer class indices. Same math, different\n   label format.\n\nQ: Why prefetch(AUTOTUNE)?\nA: Overlaps data preparation on the CPU with model execution on the GPU so the accelerator is not\n   idle between batches.\n\nQ: How do you do transfer learning in Keras?\nA: Load a pretrained base with include_top=False, freeze it, train a new head, then optionally\n   unfreeze the top blocks and fine-tune at a much lower learning rate.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Transfer learning guide', 'https://keras.io/guides/transfer_learning/', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Your Keras model needs two inputs (image + tabular metadata) merged before the output. Which model-building style?', opts: ['Sequential', 'The Functional API (or subclassing) — Sequential cannot express multiple inputs', 'A list of Sequential models', 'You must use PyTorch'], ok: 1, why: 'Sequential only supports a single linear stack. Multi-input, multi-output, shared layers, or skip connections require the Functional API or Model subclassing.' },
    { q: 'Training loss barely moves and the GPU utilisation is ~20%. tf.data likely issue?', opts: ['Learning rate too low', 'The input pipeline is the bottleneck — add .cache() and .prefetch(tf.data.AUTOTUNE), increase parallel reads', 'Too many layers', 'Wrong loss function'], ok: 1, why: 'Low GPU utilisation with slow epochs usually means the accelerator is waiting on data. Caching, prefetching, and parallel/interleaved reads keep the GPU fed.' },
    { q: 'Your labels are integers 0..9 but training errors out on shape mismatch with categorical_crossentropy. Fix?', opts: ['One-hot encode the labels OR switch the loss to sparse_categorical_crossentropy', 'Lower the learning rate', 'Add more Dense layers', 'Use MSE'], ok: 0, why: 'categorical_crossentropy expects one-hot targets; sparse_categorical_crossentropy expects integer indices. Either one-hot the labels or use the sparse variant.' } ] },

/* ============================ 12. HUGGING FACE ============================ */
{ num: 12, emoji: '🤗', title: 'Hugging Face — transformers, datasets, PEFT', layer: 'NLP / Deep Learning',
  tagline: 'The app store for pretrained models. Download one that already works; fine-tune it in a few lines.',
  apps: ['text classification / NER / summarisation', 'LLM fine-tuning with LoRA', 'the Hub: 1M+ models & datasets'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Instead of training a language or vision model, you download one from the <b>Hub</b> and use it. The <code>pipeline</code> is the 3-line on-ramp.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from transformers import pipeline\nclf = pipeline(\'sentiment-analysis\')\nclf(\'this course is unexpectedly good\')\n# [{\'label\': \'POSITIVE\', \'score\': 0.9995}]\npipeline(\'ner\', grouped_entities=True)(\'Ada Lovelace worked in London\')</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>An app store, not a compiler.</b> You are not building the model from silicon — you install one that works and configure it for your data.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Hugging Face docs', 'https://huggingface.co/docs', 'o'], ['📘 Part 2: RAG & fine-tuning decisions', '../learn2/#ch15', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The libraries:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>transformers  AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForXxx, pipeline, Trainer\ndatasets      load / stream / .map() any dataset — Arrow-backed, memory-mapped (data &gt; RAM is fine)\ntokenizers    fast Rust BPE/WordPiece/Unigram\naccelerate    device placement + multi-GPU / mixed precision, no code rewrite\npeft          LoRA / QLoRA / prefix-tuning — train a few-MB adapter on a frozen base\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\'distilbert-base-uncased\')\nmodel = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(\'distilbert-base-uncased\', num_labels=4)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b><code>AutoTokenizer</code> + <code>AutoModel</code> are universal power adapters.</b> Same two-line pattern plugs into DistilBERT, DeBERTa, Llama, ViT — the checkpoint name is the only thing that changes.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 datasets docs', 'https://huggingface.co/docs/datasets', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Two modes:</p>' +
    '<pre><code># inference\ninputs = tok(texts, padding=True, truncation=True, return_tensors=\'pt\')\nwith torch.no_grad(): logits = model(**inputs).logits\n\n# fine-tune\nfrom transformers import Trainer, TrainingArguments\nargs = TrainingArguments(\'out\', per_device_train_batch_size=16, num_train_epochs=3,\n                         eval_strategy=\'epoch\', learning_rate=2e-5, bf16=True)\nTrainer(model, args, train_dataset=ds_tr, eval_dataset=ds_val,\n        data_collator=DataCollatorWithPadding(tok), compute_metrics=metrics).train()</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Classify support tickets: <code>ds.map(tokenize, batched=True)</code>, <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification(num_labels=k)</code>, <code>Trainer</code>, push to the Hub. For an LLM: <code>peft.LoraConfig</code> + <code>get_peft_model</code> → you train ~0.1% of the parameters.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Fine-tune a pretrained model', 'https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/training', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Gotchas:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• tokenizer MUST match the model checkpoint — AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(same name)\n• padding/truncation/max_length; DataCollatorWithPadding (pad per-batch) beats pad-to-max\n• model.eval() + torch.no_grad() for inference; pipeline batches poorly by default\n• big models: device_map=\'auto\', torch_dtype=bfloat16, load_in_4bit (bitsandbytes)\n• OOM on fine-tune: LoRA/QLoRA, gradient_accumulation_steps, gradient_checkpointing, shorter seq len, DeepSpeed/FSDP\n• datasets streaming=True for corpora that do not fit on disk/RAM\n• trust_remote_code=True runs arbitrary code from the Hub repo — review it</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 PEFT (LoRA/QLoRA)', 'https://huggingface.co/docs/peft', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Build a text classifier for domain X (e.g. legal clauses). Approach?\nA: 1) Baseline: TF-IDF + LogisticRegression / LightGBM — fast, sets the bar. 2) Fine-tune a small\n   encoder (DistilBERT / DeBERTa-v3-small) with Trainer on labelled data. 3) If few labels, try\n   zero/few-shot with an instruction model or SetFit. Pick by accuracy vs latency vs cost.\n\nQ: Full fine-tune vs LoRA vs prompt/RAG — how do you choose?\nA: Prompt/RAG for knowledge & fast iteration. LoRA for behaviour/format/domain style at low cost\n   (few-MB adapter, one base). Full fine-tune only for a narrow, stable, high-volume task where\n   LoRA plateaus.\n\nQ: Why does tokenizer choice matter?\nA: The model was trained with a specific vocabulary; a mismatched tokenizer produces IDs the\n   embedding table never learned → garbage. Also affects sequence length, cost, and OOV handling.\n\nQ: AutoModel vs AutoModelForSequenceClassification?\nA: AutoModel gives the backbone (hidden states). The ForXxx variants add the task head (classifier,\n   token classifier, LM head) and the right loss.\n\nQ: GPU OOMs fine-tuning a 7B model. Options?\nA: LoRA / QLoRA (4-bit base), gradient accumulation, gradient checkpointing, shorter max_length,\n   smaller batch, bf16, DeepSpeed ZeRO / FSDP, offload.\n\nQ: How does `datasets` handle a 200GB corpus on a 16GB machine?\nA: Apache Arrow on-disk format + memory-mapping (only touched pages load) and streaming mode\n   (iterate without materialising).</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 NLP Course', 'https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'You load "roberta-base" weights but tokenize with the "bert-base-uncased" tokenizer. Likely result?', opts: ['Slightly lower accuracy but fine', 'Broken predictions — token IDs and special tokens do not match what the model learned', 'A clear exception every time', 'Faster inference'], ok: 1, why: 'Each model is tied to its tokenizer\'s vocabulary and special tokens. Feeding IDs from a different tokenizer indexes the wrong embeddings, so outputs are meaningless. Always use AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(same checkpoint).' },
    { q: 'Best default for fine-tuning a 7B LLM on one 24GB GPU for a style/format task?', opts: ['Full fine-tune in fp32', 'QLoRA: 4-bit quantised base + small LoRA adapters, with gradient checkpointing', 'Train from scratch', 'Only prompt engineering, never fine-tune'], ok: 1, why: 'QLoRA keeps the frozen base in 4-bit and trains tiny low-rank adapters, fitting a 7B fine-tune on a single consumer GPU. Full fp32 fine-tuning would need many times the memory.' },
    { q: 'Why does the `datasets` library let you .map() and iterate a dataset far larger than RAM?', opts: ['It compresses everything to 1%', 'It stores data in Apache Arrow on disk and memory-maps it (plus a streaming mode), so only the rows you touch are loaded', 'It silently subsamples', 'It requires a GPU'], ok: 1, why: 'Arrow columnar files are memory-mapped, so access pages in on demand; streaming mode iterates records without materialising the whole set. This is how 100GB+ corpora work on small machines.' } ] },

/* ============================ 13. NLP TOOLKITS ============================ */
{ num: 13, emoji: '💬', title: 'spaCy, NLTK & Gensim', layer: 'NLP',
  tagline: 'The text workshop before (and beside) transformers: fast linguistic structure, classic algorithms, topic models.',
  apps: ['entity extraction / redaction pipelines', 'text cleaning for classic ML', 'topic modelling on reviews / tickets'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Three tools, three jobs. <b>spaCy</b>: fast, production NLP — one call gives tokens, lemmas, POS, dependencies, named entities. <b>NLTK</b>: the teaching lab kit — every classic algorithm, great to learn from, slower. <b>Gensim</b>: topic models (LDA) and word/document embeddings (word2vec, doc2vec) + similarity.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import spacy\nnlp = spacy.load(\'en_core_web_sm\')\ndoc = nlp(\'Apple bought a startup in Berlin for $2B in 2019.\')\n[(e.text, e.label_) for e in doc.ents]\n# [(\'Apple\',\'ORG\'), (\'Berlin\',\'GPE\'), (\'$2B\',\'MONEY\'), (\'2019\',\'DATE\')]</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>spaCy is a power drill, NLTK is a full woodworking-class toolkit, Gensim is the lathe.</b> For a shelf you grab the drill; to learn joinery you use the class kit; to turn a bowl you need the lathe.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 spaCy 101', 'https://spacy.io/usage/spacy-101', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>spaCy processes text through a <b>pipeline</b> (tokenizer → tagger → parser → NER); each component annotates the same <code>Doc</code>.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>for tok in doc:\n    tok.text, tok.lemma_, tok.pos_, tok.dep_, tok.is_stop\ndoc.noun_chunks, doc.sents\nnlp.pipe(texts, batch_size=1000)          # batched, much faster\n\n# NLTK classics\nfrom nltk import word_tokenize, pos_tag, ngrams\nfrom nltk.stem import WordNetLemmatizer\nfrom nltk.corpus import stopwords</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>An assembly line for a sentence.</b> The raw text enters; each station bolts on an annotation (part of speech, grammatical role, entity type); the finished <code>Doc</code> rolls off with everything attached.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 NLTK book', 'https://www.nltk.org/book/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The classic tasks:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>CLEAN     lowercase, drop stopwords/punct, LEMMATISE (not stem) for readable tokens\nVECTORISE sklearn TfidfVectorizer / CountVectorizer  → sparse matrix → linear model\nNER       doc.ents; custom entities via spacy.matcher / EntityRuler, or fine-tune\nSIMILARITY spaCy md/lg vectors; Gensim KeyedVectors.most_similar(\'king\')\nTOPICS    Gensim: Dictionary + bag-of-words → LdaModel(num_topics=10); inspect top words per topic</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>50k product reviews → spaCy for lemmatised tokens + noun chunks → TF-IDF + LogisticRegression as a sentiment baseline; Gensim LDA to answer "what themes do 1-star reviews mention?" (shipping, sizing, defects).</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Gensim topic modelling', 'https://radimrehurek.com/gensim/auto_examples/tutorials/run_lda.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Gotchas:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• STEMMING mangles words: \'studies\'→\'studi\', \'better\'→\'better\'. Prefer lemmatisation for anything shown to humans.\n• spaCy \'sm\' models have NO real word vectors → use \'md\'/\'lg\' or a transformer pipeline (en_core_web_trf)\n• NLTK needs nltk.download(\'punkt\', \'stopwords\', \'wordnet\', ...)\n• naive .split() / regex tokenisation breaks on contractions, URLs, emojis, hyphenates\n• disable unused components: nlp = spacy.load(\'...\', disable=[\'parser\',\'ner\']) → 2-3x faster if you only need tokens\n• Gensim word2vec needs LOTS of text to be good; on small corpora use pretrained vectors\n• language matters — load the right model; multilingual is a separate pipeline</code></pre>' +
    '<p>Modern default: for top accuracy use a transformer (ch.12); for fast structured annotation at scale use spaCy; NLTK for learning and quick classic pieces.</p>',
    try: [['📖 spaCy: rule-based matching', 'https://spacy.io/usage/rule-based-matching', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Stemming vs lemmatisation?\nA: Stemming chops affixes with crude rules (fast, can produce non-words). Lemmatisation maps to the\n   dictionary base form using POS + a lexicon (slower, correct: \'was\'→\'be\', \'mice\'→\'mouse\').\n\nQ: Build a text-classification baseline.\nA: Clean + TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(1,2), min_df=5) + LogisticRegression / LinearSVC. Fast,\n   strong, interpretable. Then compare a fine-tuned transformer.\n\nQ: What is TF-IDF and why does it work?\nA: term frequency x inverse document frequency. Up-weights terms frequent in THIS doc, down-weights\n   terms common across ALL docs (\'the\', \'product\'). Result: a sparse vector emphasising distinctive words.\n\nQ: Limitations of bag-of-words?\nA: No word order, no meaning/synonymy, high-dimensional and sparse, out-of-vocabulary words vanish.\n   Mitigations: n-grams, embeddings, contextual transformers.\n\nQ: How does word2vec learn meaning?\nA: It trains a shallow net to predict a word from its context (CBOW) or the context from the word\n   (skip-gram); words in similar contexts get similar vectors. (Built from scratch in Part 1 ch.5.)\n\nQ: spaCy vs NLTK vs a transformer — when each?\nA: spaCy: production, speed, linguistic features, rules. NLTK: education, classic algorithms,\n   corpora. Transformer: highest accuracy on classification/NER/QA when you can afford the compute.\n\nQ: Extract custom entities (e.g. internal product codes)?\nA: Deterministic: spaCy EntityRuler / Matcher with patterns/regex. Learned: label data and fine-tune\n   a NER head.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📘 Part 1: embeddings from scratch', '../learn/#ch5', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'For a user-facing keyword summary of documents, why prefer lemmatisation over stemming?', opts: ['Stemming is slower', 'Stemming often outputs non-words (studies→studi), while lemmatisation returns real dictionary forms', 'Lemmatisation ignores stopwords automatically', 'They are identical'], ok: 1, why: 'Stemmers apply crude suffix rules and can produce fragments. Lemmatisation uses vocabulary + part-of-speech to map to the correct base form, which reads correctly to humans.' },
    { q: 'TF-IDF down-weights a term when...', opts: ['it appears many times in one document', 'it appears in a large fraction of all documents (high document frequency)', 'it is capitalised', 'it is a noun'], ok: 1, why: 'The IDF factor is inverse to how many documents contain the term, so ubiquitous words ("the", "product") get low weight while distinctive words are emphasised.' },
    { q: 'You only need tokenisation + lemmas from spaCy on 5M documents and it is slow. Best speed-up?', opts: ['Switch to NLTK regexp tokeniser', 'Load with disable=["parser","ner"] and use nlp.pipe(texts, batch_size=...)', 'Use the transformer pipeline', 'Lowercase the text first'], ok: 1, why: 'The dependency parser and NER are the expensive components. Disabling unused pipeline stages and batching with nlp.pipe gives a large throughput increase when you only need tokens/lemmas.' } ] },

/* ============================ 14. COMPUTER VISION ============================ */
{ num: 14, emoji: '🖼️', title: 'OpenCV, Pillow, torchvision & albumentations', layer: 'Vision',
  tagline: 'An image is a NumPy array of shape (H, W, 3). Everything else is array math, transforms and pretrained models.',
  apps: ['defect detection / OCR pre-processing', 'image classification & detection', 'data augmentation pipelines'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Load an image and it is just numbers: shape <code>(height, width, 3)</code>, dtype <code>uint8</code>, values 0-255. <b>Pillow</b> opens/saves/crops. <b>OpenCV</b> is the heavy toolbox (filters, edges, contours, video). <b>torchvision</b> gives datasets + pretrained models + transforms. <b>albumentations</b> is fast augmentation.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import cv2\nimg = cv2.imread(\'cat.jpg\')      # numpy array, BGR order!\nimg.shape                         # (480, 640, 3)\ngray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\ncrop = img[100:300, 50:250]       # a slice is a crop</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A photo is three stacked grayscale sheets</b> — one for red, green, blue. Blur, rotate, threshold, edge-detect: all of it is arithmetic on that stack.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 OpenCV-Python tutorials', 'https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d6/d00/tutorial_py_root.html', 'o'], ['📖 Pillow', 'https://pillow.readthedocs.io/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The array view of images:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• CHANNEL ORDER: OpenCV = BGR; Pillow / Matplotlib / torchvision = RGB  ← #1 CV bug\n• dtype uint8 (0-255); models want float in [0,1] or standardised\n• (H, W, C) for cv2/PIL  vs  (C, H, W) for PyTorch tensors\n• resize / rotate / flip / warpAffine; colour spaces HSV/LAB via cvtColor\n\nfrom PIL import Image\nim = Image.open(\'x.jpg\').convert(\'RGB\').resize((224, 224))</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>BGR vs RGB is metric vs imperial.</b> The number is the same, the label is swapped — feed BGR to an RGB-trained model and colours (and accuracy) quietly go wrong.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 torchvision transforms', 'https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/transforms.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The deep-learning image pipeline:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from torchvision import transforms as T, models, datasets\ntrain_tf = T.Compose([\n    T.RandomResizedCrop(224), T.RandomHorizontalFlip(), T.ColorJitter(0.2, 0.2, 0.2),\n    T.ToTensor(),                                  # (H,W,C) uint8 → (C,H,W) float [0,1]\n    T.Normalize([0.485, 0.456, 0.406], [0.229, 0.224, 0.225]),   # ImageNet stats\n])\nds = datasets.ImageFolder(\'data/train\', transform=train_tf)\nnet = models.resnet50(weights=\'IMAGENET1K_V2\')\nnet.fc = nn.Linear(net.fc.in_features, n_classes)   # swap the head</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>5k labelled images, 8 classes → <code>ImageFolder</code> + augmentation + fine-tune a pretrained ResNet/EfficientNet. Freeze the backbone first, train the head, then unfreeze the top blocks at a low LR. albumentations if you also need bbox/mask-aware augs and speed.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 albumentations', 'https://albumentations.ai/docs/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Gotchas:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• BGR vs RGB mismatch → wrong colours, degraded transfer-learning accuracy\n• forgetting Normalize with the SAME mean/std the pretrained model used\n• applying random augmentation to the validation/test set (use a deterministic tf there)\n• EXIF orientation: phone photos may load rotated → ImageOps.exif_transpose\n• tiny objects need higher input resolution; letterbox-resize to keep aspect ratio\n• class imbalance → WeightedRandomSampler or class-weighted loss\n• cv2.VideoCapture must be .release()d; read returns (ok, frame)\n• ToTensor scales to [0,1]; PILToTensor does not — do not double-normalise</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 torchvision models & weights', 'https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/models.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: How is an image represented, and what does ToTensor do?\nA: A uint8 array (H, W, C), 0-255. ToTensor converts to a float tensor, scales to [0,1], and\n   reorders to (C, H, W) that conv layers expect.\n\nQ: Why normalise with ImageNet mean/std for transfer learning?\nA: The pretrained weights expect inputs in the distribution they were trained on. Matching the\n   normalisation keeps activations in the range the network learned; skipping it hurts accuracy.\n\nQ: Data augmentation — why, and which augs are safe?\nA: It expands effective training data and improves generalisation/invariance. Safe augs depend on\n   the task: horizontal flip is fine for animals, NOT for digit/text recognition or "which way is\n   the arrow pointing". Never augment the eval set.\n\nQ: Where does BGR vs RGB bite you?\nA: Displaying a cv2 image with matplotlib (looks blue-tinted); feeding cv2 output straight into an\n   RGB-trained model; mixing PIL and cv2 in one pipeline.\n\nQ: 500 images per class — approach?\nA: Transfer learning from a pretrained backbone, heavy augmentation, freeze most layers, small LR,\n   strong regularisation / early stopping; consider few-shot or a frozen-feature + linear model.\n\nQ: How do you handle class imbalance in image classification?\nA: WeightedRandomSampler, class-weighted loss, focal loss, targeted augmentation of rare classes,\n   and evaluate with per-class recall / macro-F1.\n\nQ: Why convolution instead of a Dense layer for images?\nA: Local receptive fields + weight sharing → far fewer parameters, translation equivariance, and\n   a hierarchy of features (edges → textures → parts → objects).</code></pre>',
    try: [['📘 Part 1: matmul / conv intuition', '../learn/#ch3', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'You read images with cv2.imread and feed them directly into a torchvision ImageNet-pretrained model. Colours look off and accuracy is poor. Cause?', opts: ['The images are too small', 'cv2 loads BGR but the model expects RGB — convert with cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)', 'You must use JPEG only', 'ToTensor is broken'], ok: 1, why: 'OpenCV uses BGR channel order; torchvision models are trained on RGB. Feeding BGR swaps the red and blue channels, corrupting colour-dependent features.' },
    { q: 'Which augmentation must NOT be applied to your validation/test set?', opts: ['Resize to the model input size', 'Random crops, flips, and colour jitter', 'Converting to tensor', 'ImageNet normalisation'], ok: 1, why: 'Deterministic preprocessing (resize, ToTensor, Normalize) is applied everywhere. Random augmentations are training-only; using them at eval makes the metric noisy and not comparable.' },
    { q: 'Why does ToTensor() reorder an image to (C, H, W)?', opts: ['To save memory', 'PyTorch conv layers expect channels-first tensors; it also scales uint8 [0,255] to float [0,1]', 'Because RGB requires it', 'It does not — it keeps (H, W, C)'], ok: 1, why: 'torch.nn convolution operates on (N, C, H, W). ToTensor performs both the channel reorder and the [0,1] float scaling so the tensor is ready for the network.' } ] },

/* ============================ 15. TIME SERIES ============================ */
{ num: 15, emoji: '⏱️', title: 'Time Series — statsmodels, Prophet, sktime', layer: 'Time Series',
  tagline: 'Rows are a chain, not a bag: order matters, and you must never let the future leak into the past.',
  apps: ['demand / sales / traffic forecasting', 'capacity & inventory planning', 'anomaly detection on metrics'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Ordinary ML assumes rows are independent and shuffleable. <b>Time series</b> rows depend on their neighbours in time — and predicting tomorrow using tomorrow\'s data is cheating you will only discover in production.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>s = df.set_index(\'date\')[\'sales\'].asfreq(\'D\')     # regular daily index\ns.plot()\ns.rolling(7).mean().plot()                        # smooth\ns.resample(\'M\').sum()                              # monthly totals</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A diary, not a bag of cards.</b> You cannot shuffle a diary and study "Tuesday" using notes from "Wednesday". Keep the order; predict forward only.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 statsmodels tsa', 'https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/tsa.html', 'o'], ['📘 Part 1: what a sequence model sees', '../learn/#ch4', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Anatomy: <b>trend</b> + <b>seasonality</b> (daily/weekly/yearly) + cycles + noise. Many models need <b>stationarity</b> (stable mean/variance); check with the ADF test and remove trend by <b>differencing</b>.</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller\nadfuller(s)[1]                      # p-value; &lt; 0.05 → stationary\ns.diff().dropna()                   # first difference removes a linear trend\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import STL\nSTL(s, period=7).fit().plot()       # split into trend / seasonal / residual</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>Decompose a song</b> into a slow bassline (trend), a repeating beat (seasonality), and improvisation (residual). ACF/PACF plots then tell you how many AR / MA terms the residual needs.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Forecasting: Principles and Practice (Hyndman)', 'https://otexts.com/fpp3/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The models:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>CLASSICAL   statsmodels ARIMA / SARIMA / SARIMAX (with exogenous regressors),\n            ExponentialSmoothing (Holt-Winters);  pmdarima.auto_arima picks (p,d,q)\nPROPHET     additive trend + multi-seasonality + holidays; robust, few knobs, great baseline\nML / DL     sktime / darts (sklearn-style), or LightGBM on lag + calendar features,\n            statsforecast (fast classical at scale), NeuralForecast (N-BEATS, TFT)</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>Daily store sales with weekly + yearly seasonality and a promo calendar → Prophet with <code>add_country_holidays</code> + a promo regressor, or SARIMAX. Validate with a rolling-origin backtest, not one split.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Prophet', 'https://facebook.github.io/prophet/', 'o'], ['📖 sktime', 'https://www.sktime.net/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>The cardinal sins (this is the interview):</p>' +
    '<pre><code>• RANDOM train_test_split → future leaks into training. Use TimeSeriesSplit / expanding window + a gap.\n• fitting a scaler / target-encoder / feature stats on the WHOLE series → leakage. Fit on train only.\n• features built from future info (e.g. this month\'s total to predict a day in it) → target leakage.\n• one split only → use rolling-origin / backtesting; report mean + spread.\n• MAPE explodes near zero and is asymmetric → use MAE / RMSE / sMAPE / MASE.\n• multi-step forecast: recursive (feed predictions back, error compounds) vs direct (one model per horizon).\n• irregular / missing timestamps → asfreq + explicit fill; watch timezones & DST.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 sklearn TimeSeriesSplit', 'https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.model_selection.TimeSeriesSplit.html', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: How do you split time-series data for validation?\nA: Chronologically. Expanding or rolling window; train on [start..t], validate on (t..t+h];\n   optionally leave a gap = the forecast horizon to avoid leakage; average over several origins.\n\nQ: What is stationarity and why does ARIMA need it?\nA: Constant mean, variance and autocovariance over time. ARIMA models deviations around a stable\n   process; the "I" (differencing) exists to make a trending series stationary first.\n\nQ: How do you read ACF vs PACF for AR/MA orders?\nA: AR(p): PACF cuts off after lag p, ACF decays. MA(q): ACF cuts off after lag q, PACF decays.\n   Both decaying → mixed ARMA. Seasonal spikes at lag = period → seasonal terms.\n\nQ: Prophet vs ARIMA vs LightGBM-on-lags — tradeoffs?\nA: Prophet: fast, interpretable components, handles holidays/missing data, weak on complex\n   autocorrelation. ARIMA/SARIMAX: strong statistical footing, needs stationarity + order\n   selection, one series at a time. LightGBM: scales to many series, uses covariates freely,\n   needs careful lag features and leakage control, no native uncertainty.\n\nQ: Forecast demand for 10,000 SKUs?\nA: A single global model (LightGBM / a DL forecaster) on lag + calendar + price/promo features,\n   or statsforecast for fast per-series classical models; hierarchical reconciliation if totals\n   must add up.\n\nQ: Three ways future info leaks into a TS model.\nA: Shuffled split; scaler/encoder fit on all data; features computed with look-ahead\n   (rolling windows not shifted, target aggregates, "next holiday" done wrong).\n\nQ: Why is MAPE a poor metric sometimes?\nA: Undefined/explosive when actuals are near 0, and it penalises over- and under-forecasts\n   asymmetrically. Prefer MAE, RMSE, sMAPE, or MASE (scaled against a naive forecast).</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Rob Hyndman on cross-validation for time series', 'https://robjhyndman.com/hyndsight/tscv/', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'Why is a shuffled KFold split invalid for time-series forecasting?', opts: ['It is slower', 'It lets the model train on future observations and validate on past ones, leaking information and hugely overstating accuracy', 'KFold needs a target', 'It changes the seasonality'], ok: 1, why: 'Shuffling mixes time periods, so training rows can come from after the validation rows. The model effectively sees the future. Use TimeSeriesSplit / expanding windows that always train on the past.' },
    { q: 'ADF test on your series gives p = 0.40. Typical next step before ARIMA?', opts: ['Nothing, it is stationary', 'Difference the series (and/or remove trend/seasonality) and re-test until it is stationary', 'Increase the AR order to 20', 'Switch to classification'], ok: 1, why: 'A high ADF p-value means you cannot reject non-stationarity. Differencing (the "I" in ARIMA) removes trend; seasonal differencing removes seasonality. Re-run ADF until stationary.' },
    { q: 'You must forecast 10,000 product series daily. Most scalable approach?', opts: ['Fit a separate hand-tuned SARIMA per product', 'Train one global model (e.g. LightGBM or a neural forecaster) on lag + calendar + promo features across all series, with strict time-based validation', 'Average of last 3 days for everything', 'One giant VAR over 10,000 variables'], ok: 1, why: 'A single global model shares statistical strength across series, uses covariates, and scales; per-series classical models are slow to maintain at that count (statsforecast is an alternative for fast classical fitting).' } ] },

/* ============================ 16. SCALE, SERVING & MLOPS ============================ */
{ num: 16, emoji: '🚀', title: 'Scale, Serving & MLOps', layer: 'Production',
  tagline: 'Get the model out of the notebook: data bigger than RAM, reproducible experiments, and an API in front.',
  apps: ['batch scoring on 100M rows', '"which run got 0.91?" experiment tracking', 'a /predict endpoint behind a product'],
  levels: [
  { html:
    '<p>Chapters 1-15 got a model working in a notebook. This one takes it to production: process data that does not fit in memory, track experiments so results are reproducible, and serve predictions behind an API.</p>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>A recipe that works in your kitchen vs one that runs a restaurant.</b> Different problems: portioning at scale, keeping a record of every dish, and a pass-through window customers actually order from.</p></div>' +
    '<pre><code>notebook model  →  reproducible pipeline  →  tracked experiment  →  packaged artifact  →  served endpoint  →  monitored</code></pre>',
    try: [['📘 Part 2: Observability & Deterministic pipelines', '../learn2/#ch14', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Data bigger than RAM:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>Polars    multithreaded, lazy, Arrow-backed; a faster pandas (pl.scan_parquet(...).filter(...).collect())\nDuckDB    in-process SQL directly on Parquet / DataFrames; zero setup; great for analytics\nDask      parallel pandas/NumPy across cores or a cluster; familiar API, lazy graph\nPySpark   JVM cluster engine; the enterprise standard for TB-scale ETL\nPyArrow   the columnar memory format + Parquet IO they all share</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="analogy"><span class="lbl">🎯 Analogy</span><p><b>pandas is a bucket; these are a pipeline.</b> Data flows through in chunks (lazy / streaming) and you never have to hold the whole lake at once.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 Polars', 'https://pola.rs/', 'o'], ['📖 DuckDB', 'https://duckdb.org/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Reproducibility &amp; experiment tracking:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>import mlflow\nwith mlflow.start_run():\n    mlflow.log_params(params)\n    mlflow.log_metric(\'roc_auc\', score)\n    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(pipe, \'model\')      # or mlflow.autolog()\n\nWeights &amp; Biases: wandb.log({...}), sweeps, artifacts, dashboards\nDVC: git for data + pipeline stages\njoblib.dump(pipe, \'model.joblib\')  # sklearn; torch.save for PyTorch</code></pre>' +
    '<div class="reallife"><span class="lbl">📊 DS scenario</span><p>40 experiments in a week. Without tracking you cannot answer "which params + data version + code commit produced the 0.91?". With MLflow, every run has its params, metrics, the exact serialised model and the git SHA.</p></div>',
    try: [['📖 MLflow', 'https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/', 'o'], ['📖 Weights & Biases', 'https://docs.wandb.ai/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p>Serving &amp; the pitfalls:</p>' +
    '<pre><code>from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\napp = FastAPI(); model = joblib.load(\'model.joblib\')   # load ONCE at startup\nclass Req(BaseModel):\n    features: dict\n@app.post(\'/predict\')\ndef predict(r: Req):\n    return {\'p\': float(model.predict_proba(pd.DataFrame([r.features]))[:, 1])}\n\n• TRAIN/SERVE SKEW: ship the whole sklearn Pipeline so preprocessing is identical both sides\n• pin library versions — a joblib/pickle from sklearn X may not load in Y\n• ONNX / onnxruntime for framework-agnostic, fast, portable inference\n• batch vs real-time; p99 latency; cold start; input validation (Pydantic); a rollback plan\n• MONITOR: input drift, prediction drift, and delayed-label metrics; canary new models</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 FastAPI', 'https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/', 'o'], ['📖 ONNX Runtime', 'https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/', 'o']] },
  { html:
    '<p><b>Interview drill</b></p>' +
    '<pre><code>Q: Your pandas job OOMs on a 50GB CSV. Options?\nA: Convert to Parquet (columnar + compression + predicate/column pushdown); process with Polars\n   or DuckDB lazily; chunk with pandas read_csv(chunksize=); or scale out with Dask / Spark.\n\nQ: How do you make an experiment reproducible?\nA: Fix all seeds; pin dependencies (lockfile / container); version the data (DVC / a hash);\n   log params + metrics + git SHA + environment (MLflow / W&B); deterministic ops where possible.\n\nQ: How do you deploy a scikit-learn model?\nA: Serialise the entire Pipeline (preprocess + model) with joblib; load it once at service startup;\n   expose POST /predict via FastAPI with a Pydantic schema; containerise with pinned versions;\n   add input validation, logging, and a health check.\n\nQ: What is train/serve skew and how do you prevent it?\nA: Preprocessing at inference differs from training (different code, missing step, different\n   category handling). Prevent by shipping the same fitted Pipeline for both, and by testing on\n   held-out raw inputs.\n\nQ: Batch vs online inference — tradeoffs?\nA: Batch: high throughput, simple, cheap, stale between runs. Online: fresh per-request, low\n   latency needed, must handle scaling, caching, and load spikes.\n\nQ: How do you know a deployed model is degrading?\nA: Monitor input feature drift (PSI / KS), prediction distribution drift, and — once labels\n   arrive — the real metric; alert on thresholds; run a canary / shadow deployment for new versions.\n\nQ: Why Parquet over CSV?\nA: Columnar (read only needed columns), typed, compressed, splittable, with predicate pushdown —\n   often 5-50x smaller and faster to scan than CSV.\n\nQ: pandas vs Polars vs Spark — when each?\nA: pandas: fits in RAM, rich ecosystem. Polars/DuckDB: single big machine, want speed + lazy\n   execution. Spark: multi-node cluster, TB+ scale, existing data-platform.</code></pre>',
    try: [['📖 Made With ML (MLOps)', 'https://madewithml.com/', 'o'], ['📘 Part 2: Caching, Guardrails, Evals', '../learn2/#ch13', 'o']] } ],
  quiz: [
    { q: 'A scikit-learn model works in the notebook but production predictions are subtly wrong. Most common root cause?', opts: ['The model file is corrupted', 'Train/serve skew — preprocessing at inference differs from training; fix by serialising and shipping the whole Pipeline', 'FastAPI is too slow', 'The GPU is missing'], ok: 1, why: 'When preprocessing is re-implemented (or partly missing) on the serving side, inputs reach the model in a different distribution than training. Packaging the fitted Pipeline guarantees identical transforms.' },
    { q: 'You need to scan two columns out of a 200-column, 80GB dataset repeatedly. Best storage/tooling?', opts: ['Keep it as CSV and use pandas', 'Store as Parquet and query with DuckDB/Polars (columnar reads + predicate pushdown, only the needed columns/rows are loaded)', 'Load it all into RAM once', 'Convert to JSON'], ok: 1, why: 'Parquet is columnar and compressed, so a two-column scan reads a tiny fraction of the file; DuckDB/Polars push filters and projections down. CSV forces a full row-by-row parse every time.' },
    { q: 'Which set most directly tells you a live model is degrading BEFORE ground-truth labels arrive?', opts: ['Training loss curves', 'Input feature drift and prediction-distribution drift monitoring (e.g. PSI/KS), plus canary/shadow comparisons', 'The confusion matrix from training', 'Unit tests'], ok: 1, why: 'Labels are often delayed. Monitoring the distribution of incoming features and of the model\'s outputs (and comparing a canary to the incumbent) surfaces problems before you can compute the real metric.' } ] }

